    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1235">

                <pb facs="1000" n="884" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg884"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/4258.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-06-20T22:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 20, 1912—10 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I left a memorandum with the President this evening similar to my note to the
                    Minister. The President explicitly said that no bullets would be allowed to
                    penetrate American territory, and that if artillery is employed steps will be
                    taken to prevent injury to Americans. He said a siege might be employed if the
                    city is closed on the American side. The President, however, stated that no
                    troops were advancing on or near Ciudad Juárez. Note the disagreement between
                    the President’s and the Minister’s remarks.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Schuyler.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
